TONY PASTORS OPERA HOUSE,
No. 201 Bowery.—VARIETY ENTERTAINMENT at 8;
closes at 11. Matinee at 2.

The Memphis appeal says;

On the twenty-ninth day of July convention sits in Jackson, Tennessee, for the purpose of considering ways and means for the creation of a new State, consisting of the part of Kentucky and of Tennessee west of the Tennessee river, and of the district of counties of Mississippi north of the Tallahatchie river. This scheme was discussed in the newspapers of this city twenty years ago. Every county and town of the districts designated is requested to send delegates, and editors are especially invited.

The question of forming a new state out of East Tennessee has been frequently discussed since and before the war. That there is an apparent want of a harmony of interests between the grand divisions of Tennessee is manifest. An intelligent gentleman, a resident of Franklin county, remarked to us some time ago that the geographical location of Tennessee seemed a barrier to the progress of the state. The people are not in sympathy with each other as they should be, and until every grand division of the State can cooperate together for the public good, we will not move forward as we could. Whether the formation of new States is the only remedy we will not undertake to say. But the most practical presented now is the cultivation of a better state of feeling than has heretofore existed. If East Tennessee accumulates fortunes from iron, coal, marble and other minerals, that is no reason why we should despise or mistrust our neighbors of Middle Tennessee, who are or ought to be getting rich from their rich fields of cotton, corn, grass, &C., or from their fine stock. If nothing else will make the State prosper, let its territory be parcelled out among neighboring States, and let the name of Tennessee be forgotten. But this is as impracticable as it is undesirable. Then, instead of making new States, it seems to us as much better to be cultivating harmonious relations between the people of the three divisions of the State as it now stands. Nevertheless, if West Tennessee is determined to secede, we will say, so go with them. If they can do better, we certainly can do as well.

